CH = dict(num=8, tag="Polity", theme="Electoral systems, laws, Election Commission, political parties and election challenges",
 title="Elections",
 intro="Elections are the heart of democracy: they let citizens choose who will decide on their behalf and hold those leaders to account. This chapter explains why elections matter, how seats are won under different electoral systems, the laws and institutions that run Indian elections, the work of the Election Commission, the role of political parties and the challenges to free and fair polls.",
 big_questions=["Why are elections essential to a democratic system?", "How do electoral systems and rules shape representation and fairness?", "What role do institutions and laws play in ensuring free and fair elections?", "What challenges do elections face and how do they affect democracy?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "Why Do Elections Matter?"),
 ("table", ["", "Direct elections", "Indirect elections"], [
  ["Meaning", "Citizens vote directly for their representatives", "Citizens vote for representatives who then choose the leaders"],
  ["In India", "**Lok Sabha** (Centre), **Vidhan Sabha** (state), local bodies such as Panchayats and municipal corporations; every five years", "**President, Vice President, Rajya Sabha** members"]]),
 ("ul", ["A class representative (like Gurmat in Grade 8) cannot continue for ever without a fresh election; similarly a government cannot stay beyond its term without a fresh mandate, and choice is meaningless if only one party contests. Hence **periodic elections** are essential.", "Elected representatives are **accountable** to the people; the vote is the main means of making them so. Elections ensure **representation, accountability, legitimacy and equality**."]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "**Psephology** is the scientific study of elections, from Greek *psēphos* ('pebble', used for voting in ancient Greece) + *logy*."),
 ("h2", "Inside India's Election Machinery"),
 ("p", "Free, fair and transparent elections in a country with millions of voters and thousands of constituencies need laws, coordinated machinery at national, state and local levels, and stakeholders: the **Election Commission of India (ECI)**, political parties, civil society, media and voters."),
 ("h3", "The electoral system"),
 ("p", "An electoral system is the method of converting votes into seats. The Constitution makers chose the **plurality / First-Past-The-Post (FPTP)** system."),
 ("table", ["Body", "System"], [["**Lok Sabha** and **Vidhan Sabha**", "FPTP"], ["**Rajya Sabha, President, Vice President**", "Proportional representation (by single transferable vote)"], ["**Vidhan Parishad** (Legislative Council)", "Proportional representation by single transferable vote"]]),
 ("ul", ["**Vidhan Sabhas** are directly elected and make laws for states and some Union Territories. Six states have **two houses** (bicameral): Andhra Pradesh, Bihar, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Telangana and Uttar Pradesh. The **Vidhan Parishad** has members elected by MLAs and local bodies, graduates and teachers, plus some nominated by the **Governor** for art, science, social service, literature and cooperatives."]),
 ("table", ["Feature", "FPTP", "Majority (two-round) system", "Proportional representation (PR)"], [
  ["How voting works", "Vote for one candidate in a constituency; most votes wins, even below 50%", "Candidate needs **more than 50%**; if none, the top two compete again in a second round", "Voters vote for a **party**; seats allotted in proportion to votes"],
  ["Imaginary-country example (100 seats / 25 / list)", "Party A 40%, B 35%, C 25%: A wins and forms the government", "Round 1: X 45%, Y 40%, Z 15% (no one over 50%); Round 2: Y wins with 55%", "Party M 40, N 35, O 15, P 10 seats; power shared proportionately"]]),
 ("note", "FORMULA", "**Single transferable vote:** Quota = (Total valid votes ÷ (Seats to be filled + 1)) + 1. Voters mark preferences; first preferences are counted and those reaching the quota are elected; the lowest candidate is eliminated and votes move to the next preference; repeat until all seats are filled."),
 ("h3", "The laws"),
 ("table", ["Law", "Covers"], [
  ["**Representation of the People Act, 1950**", "Allocation of seats, **delimitation** of constituencies, preparation and revision of **electoral rolls**; right of every citizen above 18 to vote without discrimination"],
  ["**Representation of the People Act, 1951**", "All other aspects of conduct: nomination, campaigns, voting, resolution of disputes; lists **electoral offences and corrupt practices**"],
  ["**Presidential and Vice-Presidential Elections Act, 1952**", "Elections to the offices of President and Vice President"]]),
 ("p", "**Corrupt practices (RPA, 1951):** gifts, offers or promises to induce someone to contest, withdraw, vote or not vote; appeals for votes on grounds of religion, caste, race, community or language; taking help for the candidate from government personnel such as gazetted officers, judges and magistrates, armed forces, police and excise officers."),
 ("h3", "Delimitation Commission"),
 ("ul", ["**Delimitation** is fixing the number of seats and territorial boundaries of constituencies for the Lok Sabha and Vidhan Sabhas so that seats-to-population ratios are as equal as possible. Without it one MP might represent five lakh people and another twenty-five lakh.", "**Article 82** requires a Delimitation Commission. There have been four: **1952, 1963, 1973 and 2002**."]),
 ("h2", "Election Commission of India (ECI)"),
 ("ul", ["An **autonomous, permanent constitutional body**, established **25 January 1950**; **Articles 324 to 329** give its powers. It has superintendence, direction and control of elections to the Lok Sabha, Rajya Sabha, Vidhan Sabha, Vidhan Parishad, President and Vice President.", "In states the **Chief Electoral Officer** (appointed by the ECI from civil servants in consultation with the state government) supervises; District Magistrates, SDMs and Tahsildars act as District Election Officers, Returning Officers and Electoral Registration Officers.", "**25 January** is celebrated as **National Voters' Day** with the voters' pledge to uphold democratic traditions, free, fair and peaceful elections and to vote fearlessly, without influence of religion, race, caste, community, language or inducement."]),
 ("table", ["Function of ECI", "Details"], [
  ["**Prepares the electoral roll**", "Enumerators visit households; rolls are made constituency-wise and polling-station-wise; only listed persons may vote. **Special Intensive Revision (SIR)**: updates, verifies and corrects rolls so that no eligible citizen (especially new 18-year-olds) is left out and no ineligible person is included; removes names for death, change of residence, duplicate entry, untraceable voters; claims and objections are invited and settled before the final roll"],
  ["**Fixes the election schedule**", "Acts when the five-year term ends or the House is dissolved early; considers weather, agricultural cycle, school and university exams, festivals"],
  ["**Registers parties and allots symbols**", "Only registered parties may contest; independents can also contest. Insists on inner-party democracy (periodic organisational elections); classifies parties as national, state/regional or registered-unrecognised; allots symbols; acts as a **quasi-judicial** body in disputes over recognition and symbols"],
  ["**Ensures free and fair elections**", "Inclusive, impartial and trustworthy process; Model Code of Conduct; EVMs and VVPAT; awareness campaigns"]]),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**ETPBS** (Electronically Transmitted Postal Ballot System): lets **service voters** receive and cast postal ballots electronically from outside their constituency."),
 ("h3", "Inclusion and technology"),
 ("ul", ["Focus on persons with disabilities (PwDs), senior citizens, transgender persons and Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups (PVTGs). **Voting from home** for those above **85 years** and PwDs with **40% benchmark disability** was extended nationwide for the first time in the **2024 General Elections**. EVMs with **Braille**; ramps, wheelchairs and volunteers."]),
 ("table", ["App / system", "Purpose"], [["**Voter Helpline App**", "Registration, transposition, verification, information"], ["**Saksham App**", "Services for PwDs: registration, finding the polling station"], ["**cVIGIL**", "Report Model Code of Conduct violations in minutes; flying squads act using auto-location"], ["**Suvidha**", "For candidates and parties: nomination forms and permissions (rallies, vehicles, loudspeakers) with real-time status"], ["**Sugam**", "Managing vehicles requisitioned for election work"], ["**ERONET**", "Seamless processing of forms and electoral rolls (e-Rolls)"], ["**ETPBS**", "Electronic postal ballots for service voters"]]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "**International Election Visitors' Programme (IEVP)**: ECI's flagship for cooperation with election bodies of other countries; MoUs with **28 EMBs** and three organisations: **IFES, International IDEA and the United Nations**. India has over 96.8 crore eligible voters."),
 ("h2", "Political Parties"),
 ("p", "Parties present policies and vision, help voters learn about candidates and issues, choose the election agenda and work at the grassroots. They:"),
 ("ul", ["organise public opinion and campaign on issues;", "offer voters meaningful choices;", "enable informed decisions through rallies, manifestos and debate;", "contest elections, form the government (winning party) or sit as the opposition, formulate policies and ensure accountability in debates and committees."]),
 ("p", "India has a **multi-party system** reflecting its diversity."),
 ("h3", "Defection and the Anti-Defection Law"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Defection:** abandoning the party on whose ticket one was elected, by joining another party or acting against it (e.g. in voting). It may be seen as opportunism (against party discipline and stability) or as following conscience."),
 ("ul", ["**Anti-Defection Law:** passed in **1985** by the **52nd Constitutional Amendment Act** to give stable governments. A member of Parliament or a State Assembly who voluntarily gives up party membership or votes against the party **whip** can be disqualified; the **Speaker or Chairman** decides."]),
 ("h3", "Recognition of parties"),
 ("table", ["Party type", "Criteria (any one)"], [
  ["**National party**", "6% of valid votes in any four or more states in a Lok Sabha/Assembly election **plus** four Lok Sabha seats; **or** 2% of Lok Sabha seats from at least three states; **or** recognised as a state party in at least four states"],
  ["**State party**", "6% of valid votes in the state in a general election to the Assembly **plus** two Assembly seats; **or** 6% of valid votes in a Lok Sabha election from the state **plus** one Lok Sabha seat; **or** 3% of Assembly seats (or three seats, whichever is more); **or** one Lok Sabha seat for every 25 seats (or fraction) allotted to the state; **or** 8% of valid votes in the state in a Lok Sabha or Assembly election"],
  ["**Registered unrecognised (RUPP)**", "Parties that have not secured enough votes, or have never contested since registration"]]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "**Alliance politics:** 1967 ended single-party dominance and began an era of alliances. In **1977** the **Janata Party** formed the first coalition government at the Centre after the Emergency (1975–77). Since the late 1990s the **NDA** (led by the BJP) and the **UPA** (led by the Congress; dissolved in 2023 and reconstituted as the **INDIA** alliance) are the major alliances."),
 ("h2", "Challenges to Free and Fair Elections"),
 ("ul", ["Conducting elections for over 96.8 crore voters (2024) across thousands of polling stations and hundreds of parties is difficult. Challenges include **misinformation, fake news, intimidation**, money power and others.", "Responses: **RPA 1950 and 1951**, **Model Code of Conduct**, **EVMs**, **VVPAT** (Voter Verifiable Paper Audit Trail), voter-awareness campaigns, vigilance apps. Active citizen participation makes elections more representative and democracy stronger."]),
 ("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
  ("Why are periodic elections important?", "They make rulers accountable, give legitimacy and equal voice, and allow people to change governments."),
  ("Distinguish direct from indirect elections with examples.", "Direct: Lok Sabha, Vidhan Sabha, local bodies. Indirect: President, Vice President, Rajya Sabha."),
  ("What is psephology?", "Scientific study of elections, from Greek psephos (pebble)."),
  ("Explain the FPTP system.", "Candidate with the most votes in a constituency wins even without 50%. Used for Lok Sabha and Vidhan Sabha."),
  ("How does proportional representation differ from FPTP?", "PR gives parties seats in proportion to votes; used for Rajya Sabha, President, Vice President, Vidhan Parishad."),
  ("What does the Representation of the People Act 1950 provide? And 1951?", "1950: seats, delimitation, electoral rolls, adult franchise. 1951: conduct of elections, offences, disputes."),
  ("What is delimitation? Who conducts it?", "Fixing seats and constituency boundaries to equalise representation; Delimitation Commission under Article 82 (1952, 1963, 1973, 2002)."),
  ("Write a note on the Election Commission of India.", "Autonomous constitutional body (25 Jan 1950, Articles 324–329) that prepares rolls, schedules elections, registers parties, allots symbols and ensures fairness."),
  ("What is SIR?", "Special Intensive Revision updates and corrects electoral rolls so no eligible voter is left out and no ineligible one is included."),
  ("Name ECI initiatives for inclusion.", "Voting from home for 85+ and 40% PwDs (2024), Braille EVMs, Saksham, ETPBS, Voter Helpline, cVIGIL."),
  ("What is cVIGIL?", "App to report Model Code of Conduct violations; flying squads respond using auto-location."),
  ("What is the Anti-Defection Law?", "52nd Amendment (1985): members who leave their party or defy the whip may be disqualified by the Speaker or Chairman."),
  ("State two criteria for recognition as a national party.", "6% votes in four states plus four Lok Sabha seats; or 2% Lok Sabha seats from three states; or state-party status in four states."),
  ("What roles do political parties play in elections?", "Organise opinion, offer choices, campaign, form government or opposition, make policy."),
  ("What are the challenges to free and fair elections and remedies?", "Misinformation, fake news, intimidation, money power; remedies: RPA, Model Code of Conduct, EVM, VVPAT, awareness."),
 ]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Elections are the soul of democracy: they ensure accountability, representation, legitimacy and equality.", "India uses **FPTP** for the Lok Sabha and Vidhan Sabha, and proportional representation (single transferable vote) for the Rajya Sabha, President, Vice President and Vidhan Parishad.", "The **RPA 1950** and **1951** and the **Delimitation Commission** structure fair elections.", "The **ECI** (Articles 324–329) prepares rolls, schedules polls, registers parties, allots symbols and promotes inclusion through technology.", "Political parties inform voters and form governments or opposition; the Anti-Defection Law (1985) gives stability.", "Misinformation, fake news and intimidation challenge fair elections; vigilant citizens and the ECI address them."])
